{
    // Split-inlet scalar-mixing sensitivity case.
    //
    // Binary scalar (0 on the lower half of the inlet, 1 on the upper half,
    // expressed via the built-in `step()` expression function -- no
    // user.f90 needed) advects through a Brinkman design region and is read
    // out at the outlet by THREE scalar_mixing objectives:
    //   1. the lower-outlet quarter (y in [0, 0.25]), phi_ref = 0
    //   2. the upper-outlet quarter (y in [0.75, 1]), phi_ref = 1
    //   3. the whole outlet,                          phi_ref = 0.5
    // Advection-dominated transport keeps 1 and 2 near zero (the outer
    // quarters retain their inlet values) while 3 is large; a diffusive
    // regime drives everything toward 0.5.
    //
    // Pe was gated on the measured outflow distribution, not assumed: a
    // 4-point Pe study at this mesh/end_time (2, 20, 200, 2000) showed
    // Pe=2 and Pe=20 both already diffusively saturated (objectives 1/2
    // ~0.10-0.14, objective 3 ~2e-4-8e-4: the outlet is nearly fully
    // mixed to 0.5 regardless of the split), Pe=200 clearly
    // advection-dominated (objectives 1/2 ~1e-3, objective 3 ~0.074: the
    // quarters retain their inlet extremes), and Pe=2000 numerically
    // unstable at this dt (scalar residual diverged to ~1e6). Pe=200 is
    // therefore the lowest tested value that is both physically in the
    // intended regime and numerically stable -- see this session's agent
    // report for the full table and the recommendation reasoning.
    //
    // phi_ref of 0 and 1 are maximally off-centre, so this case leans hard
    // on the backlog-#40 fix (b37ec1e on this branch) that made "phi_ref"
    // readable at all -- on any commit before that fix every one of these
    // three objectives would have silently run at the phi_ref=0.5 default,
    // making objectives 1 and 2 identical to 3. This case is the only
    // committed regression test for that fix.
    //
    // Mesh: non-periodic in y (unlike the other nine cases in this
    // directory, which share a periodic-y box) so the outlet actually has
    // a y in [0,1] extent to split into quarters -- generated as its own
    // 26x13-element box by run_sensitivity_regression_check.sh, since it
    // cannot share the shared periodic-y box (geometry differs) or that
    // box's coarser resolution (see below).
    //
    // RESOLUTION (moved here from the unit tier, which could not certify
    // this case at all -- TOLERANCE_UNREACHABLE, tol_min = 0.902, the FD
    // estimate not converging with eps across nearly a decade):
    //
    // polynomial_order was raised to 5 first, matching every sibling case
    // in this directory (dissipation.case, passive_scalar.case, ...) --
    // per this session's brief, a spectral-element code gets far better
    // dispersion behaviour per degree of freedom from order than from `h`
    // for advection-dominated transport, so order was the first lever, not
    // the last resort. At the *shared* mesh resolution the other cases use
    // (16x8 elements, h=0.125), P=5's minimum GLL spacing is
    // 0.234944676/2 * 0.125 = 0.01468 (ratio for the N=5 GLL nodes, see
    // below), giving a cell Peclet of 200*0.01468 = 2.94 -- close to the
    // ~2 target but not under it. A *modest* additional mesh bump (16x8 ->
    // 26x13, i.e. ~1.6x linear, ~2.7x elements -- not the large jump that
    // would be needed from `h` alone without the order increase) closes
    // the gap: minimum GLL spacing at P=5 is 0.234944676070535/2 * h
    // (0.234944676070535 is the smallest of the 6 GLL node spacings on
    // [-1,1] for a 6-point/order-5 element; halved because it maps a
    // length-2 reference interval), and at h = 2/26 = 0.076923 that is
    // 0.0090363, for a cell Peclet of 200*0.0090363 = 1.807 -- comfortably
    // under 2. Kept at P=5 rather than pushed further, and at this modest
    // an N, because the explicit viscous term that actually limits the
    // time step scales as nu*dt/dx_min^2 (see next paragraph), so a finer
    // mesh or a higher order tightens that limit quadratically while
    // buying only accuracy per DOF.
    //
    // dt and time_order: the derivation that used to sit here reasoned
    // from an advective CFL of ~0.11 and concluded that the fine mesh
    // forced dt = 2.5e-4. That was the wrong constraint. A root-cause
    // investigation -- reproduced in PURE NEKO, with no Neko-TOP in the
    // loop -- found the blowup is not advective at all, but comes from the
    // one explicitly-treated viscous term in pnpn:
    //
    //   fluid_pnpn.f90:701 builds the extrapolated velocity u_e by EXT
    //   extrapolation at order case.numerics.time_order, and
    //   pnpn_res_cpu.f90:80-96 takes its double curl and scales it by
    //   mu/rho into the pressure Poisson right-hand side. That
    //   nu*curl(curl(u_e)) term is the Karniadakis-Israeli-Orszag
    //   high-order pressure boundary condition, and it is the ONLY place
    //   nu appears explicitly -- everywhere else in pnpn the viscosity is
    //   implicit, inside the Helmholtz solve, and so unconditionally
    //   stable.
    //
    // The binding number is therefore viscous, and it lives at the
    // boundary: nu*dt/dx_min^2 = 12.2 here (Re = 1, so nu = 1, against the
    // minimum GLL spacing derived above), not the advective CFL of 0.11.
    // An explicit term sitting at 12.2 survives at all only because the
    // EXT extrapolation feeding it is damped at low order; the EXT gain is
    // 1, 3, 7 at time_order 1, 2, 3, so stability degrades sharply with
    // extrapolation order rather than with dt alone.
    //
    // Four measurements pin the mechanism, all at the resolution above:
    //   * time_order 3 diverges at step 271;
    //   * time_order 2 and time_order 1 both run clean;
    //   * time_order 3 at Re = 10 (nu 10x smaller) runs clean;
    //   * time_order 3 on a periodic-y box runs clean -- no y wall, hence
    //     no boundary for the KIO term to act on.
    // Lowering Re, or removing the wall, fixes it; lowering dt alone only
    // postpones it. Hence time_order = 2 here, and dt back at the ordinary
    // 1.0e-3 the rest of the suite uses.
    //
    // Margin at time_order 2 was measured, not assumed: stable at
    // dt = 1e-3, 2e-3 AND 4e-3, i.e. 4x headroom, where time_order 3 has
    // none whatsoever at 1e-3. Dropping the extrapolation order costs this
    // case nothing: it is a steady-state case driven to convergence by the
    // `steady` simcomp, so the temporal order affects only the path to the
    // converged state, not the converged answer the objectives read.
    //
    // Why this case alone: the five sibling cases registered in this suite
    // that carry an identical Re = 1 / time_order = 3 /
    // polynomial_order = 5 / timestep = 1e-3 combination
    // (viscous_dissipation, brinkman_dissipation, dissipation,
    // dissipation_weights and passive_scalar) are not stable by virtue of
    // any better-chosen number -- they survive because they all sit on the
    // shared periodic-y box, where y has no wall and the KIO term never
    // bites. scalar_mixing is the suite's only non-periodic-y case (it
    // needs a real y in [0,1] outlet to split into quarters), and is
    // therefore the only one exposed to this. Do not "fix" the siblings by
    // copying time_order 2 across; equally, any future non-periodic-y case
    // added to this directory inherits this constraint.
    //
    // Retained from the superseded derivation, because it is an
    // independent measured finding and still true: a JSON-only fix
    // (case.time.variable_timestep) was tried and rejected because
    // Neko-TOP's own simulation driver (sources/simulation/simulation.f90)
    // passes the *whole* case params object to time_step_controller%init
    // instead of the case.time subtree Neko's own neko.f90 extracts first,
    // so every variable-timestep key is looked up at the wrong JSON scope
    // and silently falls back to its Fortran default (huge()) -- confirmed
    // by the printed dt jumping to a nonsensical 1.0 rather than honouring
    // max_timestep. That is a Neko-TOP bug worth its own backlog entry,
    // not something to work around inside this case file.
    //
    // end_time: MEASURED, not extrapolated. A single forward solve was
    // run out to t = 200 at exactly this resolution/dt/time_order and the
    // scalar residual column of steady_state_data.csv was read directly.
    // The result: after the fluid settles (fluid residuals below 5.1e-13
    // for all t > 1, i.e. six orders under the 1e-6 tolerance), the
    // scalar relaxes as a single clean exponential with a half-life of
    // 11.343 time units -- constant to four significant figures over
    // t = 20->100, 100->180 and 150->182.75, so this is one eigenmode,
    // not the "multi-eigenmode relaxation with a half-life converging to
    // ~1.0" the superseded note here claimed. That old figure was fitted
    // at t ~ 1.0, still deep in the pre-asymptotic transient, which is
    // why it was out by an order of magnitude.
    //
    // The slow mode is physical and identifiable: transverse diffusion
    // across the y extent, which is walled (Neumann) on both sides, decays
    // at alpha*pi^2 = (1/Pe)*pi^2 = (1/200)*pi^2 = 0.0493, i.e. a
    // half-life of 14.1 -- the same order as the measured 11.343, the
    // difference being advective washout through the outlet. It is not an
    // artefact of dt, the mesh or the Brinkman ball.
    //
    // The residual crosses 1e-6 at t = 182.773 (step 182773): the run
    // reached 1.0014e-6 at t = 182.750 and the remaining 0.14% is 23
    // further steps at the measured local rate. So the previous
    // end_time = 20 was four orders of magnitude short -- it stopped at a
    // scalar residual of 2.09e-2 -- and the objectives were being read off
    // a scalar field still in full relaxation. That is exactly the trap
    // backlog #24 describes, and it was live in this case.
    //
    // end_time is set to 185.0 rather than 182.8: the FD sweep evaluates
    // the case once per perturbation with a perturbed design, and those
    // trajectories need to converge too, not just the baseline's. 2.2 time
    // units is ~0.2 of a half-life of margin and costs ~2200 steps a
    // solve.
    //
    // COST: MEASURED at 2 MPI ranks (RECORD THE RANK COUNT alongside any
    // measurement here, since dFdx has been observed to differ by ~5.6e-7
    // between rank counts in this suite). One forward solve to t = 183
    // took 18447 s (5.1 h) for 182750 steps, i.e. 0.101 s/step under
    // shared load; an uncontended earlier measurement of the same solve
    // gave 0.072 s/step. A full FD run is 6 forward solves (baseline, 4
    // perturbations, 1 baseline-restore) plus one adjoint, so roughly 26
    // to 36 h. Note what this means: dropping dt from 2.5e-4 to 1.0e-3
    // removed 4x the steps, but the honest end_time went UP by 9x, so the
    // case did NOT get cheaper -- it got CORRECT. The old ~30-33 h run
    // never converged the scalar at all.
    //
    // The one large lever not pulled here, recorded because it is
    // measured: `scalar_coupled: true` on the steady simcomp means the
    // fluid is only frozen once BOTH fields converge, so the fluid keeps
    // being solved for all 183000 steps despite sitting at 5e-13 from
    // t ~ 0.35. The per-step split is 0.0470 s fluid against 0.0180 s
    // scalar, so `scalar_coupled: false` would freeze the fluid at
    // t ~ 0.35 and cut a solve to roughly 1.5 h and the sweep to ~10 h.
    // That is a change to what this case asserts about coupling, so it is
    // flagged rather than applied.
    //
    // A higher MPI rank count was tried and rejected for a measured
    // reason: this container's /dev/shm is capped at 64 MiB, and OpenMPI's
    // vader BTL requests a 16 MiB sm_segment per rank, so 8 ranks (128
    // MiB) cannot fit regardless of stale-segment cleanup. Measured
    // directly: an 8-rank run of this exact case failed after 123.6s with
    // repeated "not enough space" warnings, not a clean FD-tolerance
    // verdict -- see CMakeLists.txt.
    "version": 1.0,
    "case": {
        "mesh_file": "scalar_mixing_mesh/box.nmsh",
        "output_checkpoints": false,
        "output_at_end": false,
        "output_boundary": false,
        "time": {
            "end_time": 185.0,
            "timestep": 1.0e-3
        },
        "numerics": {
            "time_order": 2,
            "polynomial_order": 5,
            "dealias": true
        },
        "fluid": {
            "scheme": "pnpn",
            "Re": 1.0,
            "initial_condition": {
                "type": "uniform",
                "value": [ 1.0, 0.0, 0.0 ]
            },
            "velocity_solver": {
                "type": "cg",
                "preconditioner": {
                    "type": "jacobi"
                },
                "absolute_tolerance": 1e-16,
                "max_iterations": 50
            },
            "pressure_solver": {
                "type": "gmres",
                "preconditioner": {
                    "type": "hsmg"
                },
                "absolute_tolerance": 1e-12,
                "max_iterations": 50
            },
            "boundary_conditions": [
                {
                    "type": "velocity_value",
                    "value": [ 1.0, 0.0, 0.0 ],
                    "zone_indices": [ 1, 3, 4 ]
                },
                {
                    "type": "outflow",
                    "zone_indices": [ 2 ]
                }
            ],
            "output_control": "never"
        },
        "scalar": {
            "enabled": true,
            "Pe": 200.0,
            "solver": {
                "type": "cg",
                "preconditioner": {
                    "type": "jacobi"
                },
                "absolute_tolerance": 1e-18,
                "max_iterations": 50
            },
            "initial_condition": {
                "type": "expression",
                "value": "step(y - 0.5)"
            },
            "boundary_conditions": [
                {
                    "type": "expression_dirichlet",
                    "value": "step(y - 0.5)",
                    "zone_indices": [ 1 ]
                },
                {
                    "type": "neumann",
                    "flux": 0.0,
                    "zone_indices": [ 2, 3, 4 ]
                }
            ]
        },
        "adjoint_scalar": {
            "enabled": true,
            "boundary_conditions": [
                {
                    "type": "dirichlet",
                    "value": 0.0,
                    "zone_indices": [ 1 ]
                },
                {
                    "type": "neumann",
                    "flux": 0.0,
                    "zone_indices": [ 2, 3, 4 ]
                }
            ],
            "initial_condition": {
                "type": "uniform",
                "value": 0.0
            }
        },
        "adjoint_fluid": {
            "initial_condition": {
                "type": "uniform",
                "value": [ 0.0, 0.0, 0.0 ]
            },
            "boundary_conditions": [
                {
                    "type": "no_slip",
                    "zone_indices": [ 1, 3, 4 ]
                },
                {
                    "type": "outflow",
                    "zone_indices": [ 2 ]
                }
            ]
        },
        "simulation_components": [
            {
                "type": "steady",
                "tol": 1e-6,
                "compute_control": "tsteps",
                "compute_value": 1,
                "scalar_coupled": true
            }
        ],
        "point_zones": [
            {
                "name": "optimization_domain",
                "geometry": "box",
                "x_bounds": [ 0.2, 0.8 ],
                "y_bounds": [ 0.2, 0.8 ],
                "z_bounds": [ -1.0, 1.0 ]
            },
            {
                "name": "initial_ball",
                "geometry": "cylinder",
                "start": [ 0.5, 0.5, -0.5 ],
                "end": [ 0.5, 0.5, 1.5 ],
                "radius": 0.25
            },
            {
                "name": "outlet_low",
                "geometry": "box",
                "x_bounds": [ 1.8, 2.0 ],
                "y_bounds": [ 0.0, 0.25 ],
                "z_bounds": [ -1.0, 1.0 ]
            },
            {
                "name": "outlet_high",
                "geometry": "box",
                "x_bounds": [ 1.8, 2.0 ],
                "y_bounds": [ 0.75, 1.0 ],
                "z_bounds": [ -1.0, 1.0 ]
            },
            {
                "name": "outlet_full",
                "geometry": "box",
                "x_bounds": [ 1.8, 2.0 ],
                "y_bounds": [ 0.0, 1.0 ],
                "z_bounds": [ -1.0, 1.0 ]
            }
        ]
    },
    "optimization": {
        "fd_test_strict": true,
        // PROVISIONAL -- deliberately far tighter than any plausible bias,
        // so that this run's own FD_verdict_scalar_mixing.csv row measures
        // C_hat honestly (as a RED TOLERANCE_UNREACHABLE/FLOOR_EXCEEDED
        // result) rather than happening to pass at an unexamined value.
        // Once this run completes, re-derive the real tolerance as ~2x the
        // measured C_hat (see readme.md's "Tolerances of the four unit
        // cases" for the convention) and re-run for the green half of
        // red-then-green -- both require re-running the sweep, since the
        // strict verdict is computed live inside this same driver
        // invocation rather than as a post-process of the CSV. DO NOT ship
        // this value as the final tolerance.
        "fd_test_tolerance": 1.0e-6,
        // Minimal viable sweep for the strict criterion at this case's
        // cost: 4 points is the floor for the order check ("needs at
        // least 4 sweep points"), spanning 0.3 down to 0.01 (geometric-ish,
        // ratio ~3) to bracket a plateau or a truncation run without
        // committing to the historical eight-point sweep, which at this
        // case's per-perturbation cost (a full ~80000-step forward solve
        // each) would roughly double an already multi-hour run. Re-examine
        // once C_hat is known: if the sweep at this resolution behaves
        // like the FIT-branch siblings (viscous/brinkman_dissipation) a
        // 4-point sweep should be enough to measure p_hat and C_hat
        // cleanly; if it reproduces the old unit-tier's non-convergent
        // signature, widen it (accepting the added cost).
        "fd_test_perturbations": [
            3.0e-1, 1.0e-1, 3.0e-2, 1.0e-2
        ],
        "domain": {
            "type": "point_zone",
            "zone_name": "optimization_domain"
        },
        "design": {
            "type": "brinkman",
            "mapping": [
                {
                    "type": "PDE_filter",
                    "r": 0.05,
                    "tol": 1.0e-20,
                    "max_iter": 600
                },
                {
                    "type": "RAMP",
                    "f_max": 100.0
                }
            ],
            "initial_distribution": {
                "type": "point_zone",
                "base_value": 0.0,
                "zone_value": 1.0,
                "zone_name": "initial_ball"
            }
        },
        "solver": {
            "type": "mma",
            "max_iterations": 0,
            "tolerance": 1.0e-3,
            "mma": {
                "max_iter": 100,
                "asyinit": 0.5,
                "asyincr": 1.2,
                "asydecr": 0.7,
                "scale": 1.0,
                "auto_scale": false,
                "xmin": 0.0,
                "xmax": 1.0,
                "a0": 1.0,
                "a": 0.0,
                "c": 1000.0,
                "d": 0.0
            }
        },
        "objectives": [
            {
                "type": "scalar_mixing",
                "mask_name": "outlet_low",
                "phi_ref": 0.0,
                "weight": 1.0
            },
            {
                "type": "scalar_mixing",
                "mask_name": "outlet_high",
                "phi_ref": 1.0,
                "weight": 1.0
            },
            {
                "type": "scalar_mixing",
                "mask_name": "outlet_full",
                "phi_ref": 0.5,
                "weight": 1.0
            }
        ]
    }
}
